---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfMultiplePagesPerSheet from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfMultiplePagesPerSheet.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-multiple-pages-per-sheet">
  <PdfMultiplePagesPerSheet client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>N-up printing, baked into the file</h2>
      <p>
        Every desktop print driver can put several pages on a sheet, and every one of them hides the
        setting somewhere different, forgets it between jobs, and gives you no way to check the
        result before paper comes out. Producing the N-up layout as a PDF instead makes it part of
        the document: you can proof it, email it, print it from a phone, or hand it to a print shop
        and get exactly what you saw.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How many pages per sheet is still readable?</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Layout</th>
            <th scope="col">Grid on A4</th>
            <th scope="col">Each page becomes</th>
            <th scope="col">11 pt text reads as</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2-up</td><td>2 × 1 landscape</td><td>~70% of A4</td><td>~7.8 pt, comfortable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4-up</td><td>2 × 2 portrait</td><td>~49%</td><td>~5.4 pt, readable close up</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6-up</td><td>2 × 3 portrait</td><td>~40%</td><td>~4.4 pt, skim only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9-up</td><td>3 × 3</td><td>~32%</td><td>~3.5 pt, headings only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>16-up</td><td>4 × 4</td><td>~24%</td><td>~2.6 pt, a visual index</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The percentages assume a 10 mm outer margin and a 5 mm gap; reduce those and every figure
        improves slightly. Use 2-up for something you will actually read, 4-up for lecture handouts,
        and 9-up or 16-up as a contact sheet for finding a page rather than reading it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why the output stays sharp</h2>
      <p>
        Each source page becomes a PDF form XObject (a reusable vector description of the page) and
        goes on the sheet with a scaling matrix. No pixels are involved. Text
        remains real text, so the output is searchable and copyable, and it prints at the full
        resolution of the device rather than at whatever DPI a rasteriser would have chosen.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical consequence: 4-up output from this tool is legibly sharper than 4-up produced
        by any service that renders pages to images first, which is what most browser-based
        converters do.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Across then down, or down then across</h2>
      <p>
        In a 2 × 2 grid, &ldquo;across then down&rdquo; places pages 1 and 2 on the top row and 3
        and 4 on the bottom. &ldquo;Down then across&rdquo; places 1 and 3 in the left column and 2
        and 4 in the right. The first matches how a Latin-script reader scans a page and is the
        default; the second is what you want when the sheets will be guillotined into columns and
        stacked, which is how small booklets are cut.
      </p>
      <p>The numbered preview grid updates live so you can see which one you are getting.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Printing it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Choose <strong>actual size</strong> or 100% in the print dialog. &ldquo;Fit to page&rdquo;
          scales an already-shrunk sheet a second time.
        </li>
        <li>
          Do <em>not</em> also enable the driver&rsquo;s own multiple-pages-per-sheet option, or you
          will get 4 pages of 4 pages.
        </li>
        <li>
          Turn on the thin border if you will cut the sheet. It gives you a line to follow.
        </li>
        <li>
          For double-sided N-up, print on both sides with &ldquo;flip on long edge&rdquo; so the
          grids line up back to back.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>This is not booklet imposition</h2>
      <p>
        A saddle-stitched booklet needs pages reordered as 8-1, 2-7, 6-3, 4-5 so that folding the
        stack gives the correct sequence. That is a different operation, and this tool does not do it.
        It lays out pages in reading order. For a folded booklet, impose the pages
        first with <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a> and then run 2-up here.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
